#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198749 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ed6bf75-84fa-56c1-9e18-626b99c513e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281820} {:answer/id #uuid "266a6ff4-bf98-5301-81a2-052f525db5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282025} {:answer/id #uuid "31c825c3-5c0e-5fe3-97cb-f77e3a036d29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282137} {:answer/id #uuid "3c7824e8-1848-5392-ba1f-1d9573bb5770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282219} {:answer/id #uuid "e267522c-82f6-5771-9d4f-70a960c943bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "266a6ff4-bf98-5301-81a2-052f525db5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282025} :answer-field/id #uuid "10529c41-b955-59ab-bfeb-a9e21c91b899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70a4471c-4be9-5243-a617-e32ac5259d39" :question/math-academy-id "q-177075" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=18-18=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})$ is not a unit vector since\n\n$$\n‖Q({u}_{1})‖=‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{{9}^{2}+{6}^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19a57218-72df-52b7-a7c9-b44507555a2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281924} {:answer/id #uuid "236f86aa-fa00-5096-a10a-e7651f6a6f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282002} {:answer/id #uuid "44f8d700-4e7f-5f15-911f-34b6d469a632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282274} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2190ba-be25-51f1-8832-021adf3dcc68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282623} {:answer/id #uuid "8f930388-ab9f-54e0-8499-291a03111580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f930388-ab9f-54e0-8499-291a03111580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282934} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d6f2115-fb0f-5808-a58b-b7b09276ab3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e67e934-1049-5a47-aaca-94c1d2a3bfc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-177118" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})}$ is orthonormal" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=2+12-5=9\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Angle Preserving Property of Orthogonal Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a2dbbe-8269-5871-944e-208bd7aa03cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281693} {:answer/id #uuid "5763789f-ad02-57d2-8abb-2d3ef223624f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282431} {:answer/id #uuid "5f0011b6-dd86-54dd-93e2-86e2608cd8f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282487} {:answer/id #uuid "aa73376a-4ff1-5a4a-8b18-a94ebfa6fe03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283167} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a08606-13ff-5fb7-8e16-1af3bdc83744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f0011b6-dd86-54dd-93e2-86e2608cd8f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282487} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e6e1327-d962-5a5a-86df-5a6da163058c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eeb4fa8d-201a-5a9d-8ffa-ae311083bf29" :question/math-academy-id "q-177769" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{6})}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Angle Preserving Property of Orthogonal Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a72e896-d047-5004-a73f-afd0fbcbb77b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282058} {:answer/id #uuid "366d455d-9990-5b93-b7b2-600d62d4cc54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282172} {:answer/id #uuid "5064ac84-ef50-510c-8a43-b86cafd44145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282378} {:answer/id #uuid "72a81145-45cf-5cc8-9f62-ce693367213d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282670} {:answer/id #uuid "d2771588-d7cd-5658-8912-e696f18ee8a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2771588-d7cd-5658-8912-e696f18ee8a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283524} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b22f39-03e5-53f5-bf7c-13b93c3d9aae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a26587c8-f4e3-50c7-8549-c17141f2d144" :question/math-academy-id "q-177950" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-7)+1\\cdot (-5)+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fce5c53-c88f-5213-9b57-f76cc256d9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415150} {:answer/id #uuid "75a2a860-9b99-512b-901f-14c2d162a694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415151} {:answer/id #uuid "87e451be-c0a6-5f4f-8367-e5f2bd3d8579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415152} {:answer/id #uuid "a91a4b12-8794-5dbc-b111-cafaa0420df7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415153} {:answer/id #uuid "ac673688-1d30-5b45-8f99-0b4b41a43e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac673688-1d30-5b45-8f99-0b4b41a43e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415154} :answer-field/id #uuid "288b1ab6-6f15-58b3-8714-e1582f968b3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a7fbace-2464-5d79-be05-e6f28745c3de" :question/math-academy-id "q-178014" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=12-12=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})$ is not a unit vector since\n\n$$\n‖Q({u}_{1})‖=‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-6)^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
